A||||4||William||Reinstalling stock springs||||1||
Z||000000||William||03-31-2001||02:20 PM||||I can't get them in! The haynes book just say put them in.... No kidding that really helps. I read somewhere that a guy just kicked them in. I tried but almost kicked the car off the jack stands. I tryed using the floor jak, but the springs starts tobend the wrong way. I don't want to have to get lowering springs as I still have lots to buy for the rear! Looks like I may have to. help||||reg||1
Z||000001||MrPBody||03-31-2001||02:52 PM||||My stock front springs ('85 SE WS6) were a bugger to get out!  They were easier to reinstall after I cut a coil off them.<P>I think I did it this way:<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>remove shock absorber (Duh! -- I assume you have already done this.)<BR><LI>loosen lower control arm bolts<BR><LI>support lower control arm with a floor jack, at whatever height seems to work<BR><LI>insert upper end of spring into socket<BR><LI>with a crowbar, pry the lower end of the spring over the spring seat "hump" while pushing the spring with a foot<BR></UL><P>This probably is a hazardous way to do it, but I know no other.  Maybe you could compress the springs with a press and wire them in that position before installation.  I don't think most spring compressors will fit.  Or maybe you can remove the lower control arm, install the spring, set the control arm under it, then jack-up the control arm and spring together.<P>Anyhow, good luck!  Watch for flying springs.  Don't injure yourself because I give crappy advice.<P>Did I meet you at a Georgia Fiero Club meeting?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||03-31-2001||04:29 PM||||When we do it we push the control arm all the way down. Almost vertical to the frame. Then slide spring up onto upper mount. Attach chain around spring and frame. (Safety precaustion so spring doesn't shoot out!) Using a large long flatblade screwdriver we stick it thru the spring and lift it over the control arm hump while pulling on the control arm. Once it's hooked we start to jack up the control arm. Do not stand in front of the spring, set the jack handle off to one side. Or you could go to NAPA and rent a spring compresser.||||reg||1
Z||000003||RNGCRSH||03-31-2001||07:17 PM||||nothing like the right tool for the job.<P>get a spring compressor, any other method and you are asking for an injury.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bob Englert||03-31-2001||11:06 PM||||I have always had a hard time installing the stock springs on my SE and GT.  I found a method that has worked well for me and I believe is very safe.<P>I purchased a set of coil spring lowering clamps from JC Whitney.  These are supposed to be used to pull the springs lower on the car.  WHile I ould never use them for that purpose, they work well to temporarily hold the spring conpressed while reinstalling it in the car.<P>The part I am using can be found at <A HREF="http://www.jcwhitney.com/productnoitem.jhtml?CATID=14834" TARGET=_blank>http://www.jcwhitney.com/productnoitem.jhtml?CATID=14834</A> <P>I use regular spring compressors oriented along the axle center line to the left and right when the spring is installed in the car.  After compressing the spring at least 1 inch or more, I install the JCW lowering straps to the front and rear so they will clear the control arms.  The compressed springs will allow you to get them back in and after supporting the lower control arm with a floor jack you can remove the JCW device.<P>I have used this several times with good success.  You can install the JCW kit before removing the springs from the car which makes it much easier to get them back in.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
